{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Agenesis of the dorsal pancreas is a congenital malformation in which either the entire dorsal pancreas or part of it fails to develop (complete or partial agenesis).","source_span":"a congenital malformation of the pancreas in which either the entire dorsal pancreas or part of the dorsal pancreas fails to develop (complete agenesis or partial agenesis, respectively)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Hyperglycemia has been shown to be present in approximately 50% of individuals with agenesis of the dorsal pancreas.","source_span":"Hyperglycemia has been shown to be present in approximately 50% of affected individuals."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Symptoms of agenesis of the dorsal pancreas can include hyperglycemia, diabetes mellitus, bile duct obstruction, abdominal pain, and pancreatitis.","source_span":"others may develop hyperglycemia, diabetes mellitus, bile duct obstruction, abdominal pain, pancreatitis, or other conditions"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Agenesis of the dorsal pancreas may occur sporadically, or in some cases with suggested autosomal dominant or X-linked dominant inheritance.","source_span":"It may occur in individuals with no history of the condition in the family (sporadically) and in some cases, autosomal dominant or X-linked dominant inheritance has been suggested."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Agenesis of the dorsal pancreas has been reported to occur with the very rare conditions polysplenia and polysplenia/heterotaxy syndrome.","source_span":"It has also been reported to occur with very rare conditions including polysplenia and polysplenia/heterotaxy syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"Agenesis of the dorsal pancreas results from failure of the dorsal pancreatic bud to form the body and tail of the pancreas during fetal development.","source_span":"Partial or complete agenesis of the dorsal pancreas results from the failure of the dorsal pancreatic bud to form the body and tail of the pancreas in the developing fetus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"When associated with polysplenia/heterotaxy syndrome, agenesis of the dorsal pancreas may be linked with benign to severe congenital cardiac malformations.","source_span":"it may occur due to errors in development of the asymmetric organs and may be associated with benign to severe congenital cardiac (heart) malformations"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000183","statement":"During pancreatitis attacks, individuals should avoid pain medications such as acetaminophen that can affect the liver.","source_span":"avoiding pain medications that can affect the liver (such as acetaminophen)"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What congenital malformation is described by agenesis of the dorsal pancreas?","answer":"It is a congenital malformation in which either the entire dorsal pancreas or only part of it fails to develop, referred to as complete or partial agenesis respectively."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What percentage of people with agenesis of the dorsal pancreas experience hyperglycemia?","answer":"Approximately 50% of affected individuals have hyperglycemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What symptoms can occur in people with agenesis of the dorsal pancreas?","answer":"Symptoms can include hyperglycemia, diabetes mellitus, bile duct obstruction, abdominal pain, and pancreatitis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What inheritance patterns have been suggested for agenesis of the dorsal pancreas?","answer":"It can occur sporadically with no family history, and in some cases autosomal dominant or X-linked dominant inheritance has been suggested."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[4,6],"question":"What rare syndromes have been reported to occur alongside agenesis of the dorsal pancreas, and what additional complication can result?","answer":"It has been reported with polysplenia and polysplenia/heterotaxy syndrome, which in turn may be associated with benign to severe congenital cardiac malformations."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What developmental failure causes agenesis of the dorsal pancreas?","answer":"It results from the failure of the dorsal pancreatic bud to form the body and tail of the pancreas during fetal development."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000183","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Which common pain medication should be avoided during a pancreatitis attack because it can affect the liver?","answer":"Acetaminophen should be avoided since it can affect the liver."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000219","statement":"Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy is caused by mutations in the GNAS gene and is transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait.","source_span":"This autosomal dominantly inherited condition is caused by mutations in the GNAS gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000219","statement":"Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy features include short stature, obesity, round face, subcutaneous ossifications, and brachydactyly (shortening and widening of the bones in the hands and feet).","source_span":"a syndrome with a wide range of manifestations including short stature, obesity, round face, subcutaneous (under the skin) ossifications (gradual replacement of cartilage by bone), and characteristic shortening and widening of the bones in the hands and feet (brachydactyly)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000219","statement":"Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy is associated with resistance to parathyroid hormone (pseudohypoparathyroidism) and to other hormones, particularly thyroid-stimulating hormone.","source_span":"The features of Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy are associated with resistance to parathyroid hormone (pseudohypoparathyroidism) and to other hormones (thyroid-stimulation hormone, in particular)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000219","statement":"Treatment of Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy consists of calcium and vitamin D supplements.","source_span":"Treatment consists of calcium and vitamin D supplements."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000219","statement":"Phosphate binders such as calcium carbonate, calcium acetate, and sevelamer HCl may be used to lower high blood phosphate levels in Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy.","source_span":"medications called phosphate binders to help lower the levels of phosphate. Examples of phosphate binders include calcium carbonate, calcium acetate, and sevelamer HCl."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What gene mutation causes Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy, and how is it inherited?","answer":"It is caused by mutations in the GNAS gene and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What physical features characterize Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy?","answer":"Features include short stature, obesity, round face, subcutaneous ossifications (cartilage gradually replaced by bone), and brachydactyly — shortened, widened bones in the hands and feet."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which hormones does the body become resistant to in Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy?","answer":"Affected individuals develop resistance to parathyroid hormone (pseudohypoparathyroidism) and to other hormones, particularly thyroid-stimulating hormone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the standard treatment for Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy?","answer":"Treatment consists of calcium and vitamin D supplements."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What medications can be used to lower high phosphate levels in someone with Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy?","answer":"Phosphate binders such as calcium carbonate, calcium acetate, and sevelamer HCl can be used to lower phosphate levels."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000219","fact_refs":[0,2],"question":"Why do some family members with Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy have hormone resistance while others do not?","answer":"This depends on whether the mutated GNAS gene allele is inherited from the mother or father: a mutated maternal allele leads to full disease expression with hormone resistance, while a mutated paternal allele leads to partial expression (pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism) without hormone resistance, due to parental imprinting of the GNAS gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"Alopecia universalis is characterized by complete loss of hair on the scalp and body and is an advanced form of alopecia areata.","source_span":"Alopecia universalis (AU) is a condition characterized by the complete loss of hair on the scalp and body. It is an advanced form of alopecia areata"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"Alopecia universalis is thought to be an autoimmune condition in which the immune system mistakenly attacks the hair follicles.","source_span":"it is thought to be an autoimmune condition in which an affected person's immune system mistakenly attacks the hair follicles"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"Roughly 20% of people with alopecia universalis have a family member with alopecia, suggesting genetic factors may contribute.","source_span":"Roughly 20% of affected people have a family member with alopecia, suggesting that genetic factors may contribute to the development of AU."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"There is currently no cure for alopecia universalis, but hair regrowth sometimes occurs on its own, even after many years.","source_span":"There is currently no cure for AU, but sometimes hair regrowth occurs on it's own, even after many years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"About 40% of people treated with topical immunotherapy will regrow scalp hair after about six months of treatment.","source_span":"About 40% of people treated with topical immunotherapy will regrow scalp hair after about six months of treatment."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"Diagnosis of alopecia universalis is usually based on signs and symptoms; a scalp biopsy is rarely needed to confirm it.","source_span":"A diagnosis of alopecia universalis is usually based on the signs and symptoms present in each person. In rare cases, a scalp biopsy may be needed to confirm the diagnosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000247","statement":"Regrown hair from corticosteroid treatment is likely to fall out again when the corticosteroids are stopped.","source_span":"regrown hair is likely to fall out when the corticosteriods are stopped"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is alopecia universalis?","answer":"It is a condition characterized by complete loss of hair on both the scalp and body, and it is an advanced form of alopecia areata."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is believed to cause alopecia universalis?","answer":"It is thought to be an autoimmune condition in which the affected person's immune system mistakenly attacks the hair follicles."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What proportion of people with alopecia universalis have a family member with alopecia?","answer":"Roughly 20% of affected people have a family member with alopecia, which suggests genetic factors may contribute to the condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Is there a cure for alopecia universalis?","answer":"No, there is currently no cure, but hair regrowth sometimes occurs on its own, even after many years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What percentage of people regrow scalp hair after about six months of topical immunotherapy for alopecia universalis?","answer":"About 40% of people treated with topical immunotherapy regrow scalp hair after about six months of treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000247","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How is alopecia universalis typically diagnosed?","answer":"It is usually diagnosed based on the signs and symptoms present in the person; a scalp biopsy is rarely needed to confirm the diagnosis."}
